= Solution
{parent=i}

The <trace> sums the diagonal entries, so $\operatorname{tr}P=\sum_i1-\sum_i a_i^2-\sum_i b_i^2$. The two <unit vectors> have squared <Euclidean norms> one, giving $\boxed{\operatorname{tr}P=n-2}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $P=I-Q$ with $Q=aa^T+bb^T$. The <orthonormality> conditions $a^Ta=b^Tb=1$ and $a^Tb=b^Ta=0$ give
$$
Q^2=a(a^Ta)a^T+a(a^Tb)b^T+b(b^Ta)a^T+b(b^Tb)b^T=aa^T+bb^T=Q.
$$
Consequently $P^2=I-2Q+Q^2=I-Q=P$. It is the <orthogonal projection matrix> onto the <orthogonal complement> of the <linear span> of $a,b$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <matrix product> is $Pa=a-a(a^Ta)-b(b^Ta)=a-a=0$. Thus $\boxed{Pa=0}$, and the nonzero <vector> $a$ is in its <kernel>. Hence $P$ is singular and $\boxed{\det P=0}$. This includes $n=2$, when $a,b$ span the whole space and $P=0$.

