{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <transpose> reverses a <matrix product>. Since $S^T=S$, we have $(S^2)^T=S^TS^T=S^2$. Therefore **$S^2$ is a <symmetric matrix>**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply <trace orthogonality of symmetric and antisymmetric matrices>. In detail, invariance of the <trace> under <transpose> and its cyclic property give
$$
\operatorname{tr}(SA)=\operatorname{tr}((SA)^T)=\operatorname{tr}(A^TS^T)=-\operatorname{tr}(AS)=-\operatorname{tr}(SA).
$$
Therefore $\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(SA)=0}$. The negative sign comes from $A^T=-A$, while $S^T=S$.

